With the rise of {{ill|Honji suijaku|ja|本地垂迹説}}, these Daimyōjin were interpreted as manifestations of Buddhist {{ill|Budda|ja|仏}} appearing to save the Japanese people.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=79}} For example, the early 12th-century text {{ill|Chūkan Sen|ja|注好選}} claimed that {{ill|Shakyamuni|ja|釈迦}} appeared as "Daisha Myōjin," and referenced a supposed passage in the {{ill|Bikekyō|ja|悲華経}} stating, "After my death, during the age of the latter dharma, I will appear as a Daimyōjin to save sentient beings." While this passage is not found in the actual text of the Bikekyō, the belief became widely accepted.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=82}}

From the medieval to early modern periods, deities were rarely referred to by their original names and were instead called by titles such as Myōjin or Gongen.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} The Yoshida Shintō school institutionalized the granting of Myōjin titles to deities.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} In 1591, the court posthumously conferred the title "Toyokuni Daimyōjin" upon {{ill|Toyotomi Hideyoshi|ja|豊臣秀吉}}.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} During the Edo period, a debate arose over the posthumous title for {{ill|Tokugawa Ieyasu|ja|徳川家康}}, with {{ill|Tenkai|ja|天海}} advocating for "Daigongen" and {{ill|Ishin Sūden|ja|以心崇伝}} proposing "Daimyōjin."{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}}

