During the Heian period, deities receiving special reverence were often referred to as Myōjin or Daimyōjin. In some cases, the same deity might be called by both titles in a single source, without a clear distinction between the two.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=79-80}} Scholar {{ill|Imahori Taiichi|ja|今堀太逸|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Imahori Taiichi|qq=}} observed that deities with the divine Rank {{ill|Shin'i|ja|神位|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shin'i|qq=}} of Senior First Rank or Junior First Rank were commonly labeled as Daimyōjin.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=79}}

With the rise of {{ill|Honji suijaku|ja|本地垂迹説|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Honji suijaku|qq=}}, these Daimyōjin were interpreted as manifestations of Buddhist {{ill|Budda|ja|仏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Budda|qq=}} appearing to save the Japanese people.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=79}} For example, the early 12th-century text {{ill|Chūkan Sen|ja|注好選|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Chūkan Sen|qq=}} claimed that [[:en:The Buddha|Shakyamuni]] appeared as "Daisha Myōjin," and referenced a supposed passage in the {{ill|Bikekyō|ja|悲華経|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Bikekyō|qq=}} stating, "After my death, during the age of the latter dharma, I will appear as a Daimyōjin to save sentient beings." While this passage is not found in the actual text of the Bikekyō, the belief became widely accepted.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=82}}

From the medieval to early modern periods, deities were rarely referred to by their original names and were instead called by titles such as Myōjin or Gongen.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} The Yoshida Shintō school institutionalized the granting of Myōjin titles to deities.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} In 1591, the court posthumously conferred the title "Toyokuni Daimyōjin" upon [[:en:Toyotomi Hideyoshi|Toyotomi Hideyoshi]].{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}} During the Edo period, a debate arose over the posthumous title for [[:en:Tokugawa Ieyasu|Tokugawa Ieyasu]], with [[:en:Tenkai|Tenkai]] advocating for "Daigongen" and [[:en:Ishin Sūden|Ishin Sūden]] proposing "Daimyōjin."{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=74}}

In 1868, the [[:en:Shinbutsu bunri|Shinbutsu Bunri]] edict led to the removal of Buddhist-derived terminology from Shinto practice.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=73}} Although the term Myōjin was not explicitly banned, it was associated with Buddhism and saw decreased use.{{sfn|Nakamura Kazuharu|2009|pp=73}} However, some shrines, such as those venerating {{ill|Inari Daimyōjin|ja|稲荷大明神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Inari Daimyōjin|qq=}}, continue to use the title today.

